  "body": "**Replaces #430**, which was opened from the old fork. GitHub detached that fork from this repo's network when NeoTech/Camcraft was briefly private, so it can no longer be updated. This branch carries the merges with main through #461: the mesh law reads #381's `HolderStack` (`steel_stack(geom, holder, spindle)`, with `mesh_refined` wrapping the `*_held` laws), and #395's turning holder judge is kept. The recipe in the old 'merge note for #381' below is now done.\n\nSlice 1 of #339 (triage and split are on the issue). Fixture collisions are judged against the fixture's real mesh, so a path down a vise's gap posts clean.\n\n## What changed\n**The box laws still run first, and the mesh can only clear a hit.** When a fixture's world box flags an upright move (laws c2 and d2), the fixture's closed mesh gets the final say, and it can only clear that hit, never add one. That keeps the box laws' deliberate silences, such as a retract off the mounting face. Where nothing is cleared, output is identical to before, labels included.\n\n**Test shape.** Each tool band (flute, shank, holder, and the spindle housing when stated) is an upright cylinder swept along the move, an exact convex shape. Arcs are split into chords, each padded by how far the true arc can stray from it, plus 1 µm.\n\n**Why it can't miss a real hit:**\n- A band counts as clear only when a GJK distance test proves a gap of more than `FIXTURE_HIT_TOL` (plus the arc pad) to every nearby triangle. If the search fails or runs out, the answer is a hit.\n- A band far from every triangle could still be buried inside the solid, so a point of it is tested by ray parity. Six fixed off-axis rays are tried; any that graze an edge, vertex or face are discarded, and if all are ambiguous the point counts as inside.\n- Only closed meshes judge (each edge used once in each direction).\n- A bounding-volume tree over the triangles is pruned with the same proof at every node.\n\n**What keeps the box verdict:**\n- Meshes that are open, non-finite, fully collapsed or over 4M facets: a named `MeshError`, logged.\n- Tilted moves, moves with a non-zero C angle, and XZ/YZ arcs.\n- Lathe tool kinds and non-mill machines.\n- A box shared by two fixtures.\n\n**Performance (release):**\n- A 203k-facet closed mesh: validate and build the tree in 0.05 s.\n- Worst case, 2000 passes grazing it 0.05 mm off (every one inside its box): 0.74 s total (0.37 ms/move), all correctly cleared.\n\n**Code:**\n- New: `crates/sim/src/fixture_mesh/` (mod, gjk, bvh, ray), `crates/sim/src/verify/mesh_steel.rs`.\n- Hooks: `fixture::meshes_for`, plus small edits in `operation/{job,generate}.rs` and `posting.rs` (meshes cached once per setup).\n- `docs/decisions/339-fixture-mesh.md`\n\n**Merge note for #381:** this edits next to #381's `HolderStack` changes in `posting.rs` `fixture_problems` and `generate.rs` `workholding_warnings`. Whichever lands second resolves by keeping `mesh_refined(path, &wh, &steel, rapids, |w| <law>)` with the closures calling #381's `_held` variants, and by building `steel_stack` from the `HolderStack` bands. The decisions file has the recipe.\n\n## How tested\n- `crates/sim/tests/fixture_mesh.rs` (8 tests, plus one in the heavy lane):\n  - a vise-gap pass, plunge and arc are cleared;\n  - a jaw-corner clip still hits, while the same line 4.95 mm off is cleared;\n  - a jaw-face clip, a holder over a jaw, a rapid into a jaw top, and a tool buried in steel all still hit;\n  - open, NaN, empty and collapsed meshes refuse by name and keep the box verdict;\n  - a tilted move and a fixture-less table box keep the box verdict;\n  - timings (heavy lane: the 200k-facet case).\n- `crates/app/tests/fixture_mesh.rs` (3 tests), end to end through `posting::verification_gate` and regeneration:\n  - vise jaws 3 mm off the pass: clean;\n  - the same vise as an open sheet: box verdict;\n  - jaws 1 mm into the pass: the post is blocked and generation warns.\n- `scripts/gate.sh quick upstream/main`: sim suites green; app suites green except `setup_stock_source::a_new_block_after_a_flip…`, which is red on plain main (#415). Every existing fixture suite is unchanged.\n- `clippy -p camcraft-sim -p camcraft-app --tests -D warnings` is clean.\n\n## Not exercised / follow-ups\n- **Two P0 gaps in the existing box laws** were found by this survey and filed separately: #424 (upright rapids check only the flute) and #425 (ramps judged at their lowest z). This slice does not fix them, since the mesh only clears hits. The triage's slice 2 makes the mesh law able to add hits too.\n- The remaining #339 slices: tilted and rotary moves against the mesh, fixture UX, routing around fixtures, and a fixture library.\n- No GUI change, and no change to posted G-code. More programs now post: those whose only \"hit\" was a box around empty space between jaws.\n\nChangelog: Fixture collisions are judged against the fixture's real mesh (the box stays the broad phase), so a path between vise jaws no longer refuses the post (#339)\n\nRefs #339\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n",
